<header><p class="st-kicker">county · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Blois</h1><p class="st-meta">940 CE – 1397 CE (approx.) · capital Blois</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Loire county of the Thibaudian counts, rivals of the early Capetians, whose family gave England King Stephen and later united with Champagne.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theobald-i-of-blois">Theobald the Trickster</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-the-great">Hugh the Great</a>, held Blois, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chartres">Chartres</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a> by around 940 and founded a dynasty. His grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odo-ii-of-blois">Odo II</a> added <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troyes">Troyes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meaux">Meaux</a> around 1022, encircling the Capetian royal domain and contending with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-anjou">Anjou</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/touraine">Touraine</a>, which was lost in 1044.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The counts ruled a rich farming land of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beauce">Beauce</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chartres">Chartres</a> and its cathedral school a major centre of learning. Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-count-of-blois">Stephen-Henry</a>, husband of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adela-of-normandy">Adela of Normandy</a>, was a leader of the First Crusade, and their son Stephen became king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1135. The family's <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-champagne">Champagne</a> branch, ruling the County of Champagne, became the more powerful line.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From the 1230s the house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-chatillon">Châtillon</a> held Blois. The last count, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-ii-of-blois">Guy II</a>, sold it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-duke-of-orleans">Louis of Orléans</a> in 1391, with possession at his death in 1397. When Louis's grandson, born at Blois, became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xii-of-france">Louis XII</a> in 1498, it joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-blois.jpg" alt="County of Blois" width="320" height="365"><figcaption>Image: Thibaud4141 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_France_1030-en2.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>940 CE – 1397 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Blois</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Theobald the Trickster, Odo II of Blois, Stephen-Henry of Blois</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>France</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Blois, Blois-Chartres</dd></dl></aside></div>
